WEBVTT

00:03.470 --> 00:06.060
Sumireko-san, do you have a moment?

00:06.060 --> 00:06.860
Hm?

00:06.860 --> 00:10.240
We have a customer looking for 
this title. Do you recognize it?

00:09.900 --> 00:10.870
<b></b>Farting: the Complete

00:10.870 --> 00:12.720
<i>Farting: the Complete Collection</i>?

00:13.450 --> 00:16.550
It should be over there on 
the culture and folktales shelf.

00:16.550 --> 00:19.200
Huh? It's an actual book?

00:19.970 --> 00:21.170
Is this the one?

00:21.170 --> 00:22.630
Yes, it is!

00:22.630 --> 00:24.880
This is the one! I'm so glad!

00:25.310 --> 00:27.170
Thank you.

00:27.840 --> 00:29.180
You really saved me.

00:29.180 --> 00:31.200
You can thank me with a can of coffee.

00:31.200 --> 00:33.140
Working in a bookstore makes me realize

00:33.140 --> 00:38.070
how many books are out there 
that I've never heard of.

00:38.070 --> 00:41.400
And there are more being published every day.

00:41.400 --> 00:42.860
I just can't get enough.

00:42.860 --> 00:44.650
But, Sumireko-san,

00:45.100 --> 00:48.270
you know pretty much every 
book in this store, don't you?

00:48.270 --> 00:49.340
It's creepy.

00:49.960 --> 00:52.310
You don't get to diss me!

00:52.310 --> 00:54.280
That's one more can of coffee.

00:54.280 --> 00:54.910
What?

00:56.400 --> 00:59.030
I can't help being obsessive about books.

00:59.030 --> 01:01.480
There are some I've been looking for forever.

01:01.480 --> 01:03.670
In my search, I end up remembering others.

01:03.670 --> 01:07.790
"Forever"? How long have you 
been unable to find them?

01:08.670 --> 01:10.890
A really long time.

01:10.890 --> 01:14.680
I read them so many times, 
but I can't remember the titles.

01:15.310 --> 01:19.490
The only lead I have is this feeling 
that they were really absorbing.

01:19.490 --> 01:23.370
Looking for them is one reason 
I work in a bookstore.

01:23.370 --> 01:25.950
Isn't it just that your memory's lacking?

01:26.690 --> 01:27.920
Silence, boy!

01:27.920 --> 01:29.650
That's three cans of coffee now!

01:34.950 --> 01:36.950
Welcome.

01:36.950 --> 01:38.840
To stay alive and return home...

01:39.210 --> 01:41.460
That's our goal as siblings.

01:42.330 --> 01:43.460
<i>I see.</i>

01:44.140 --> 01:47.000
<i>One day, you'll be gone, too.</i>

01:51.590 --> 01:56.390
<b>Mysterious Disappearances</b>

01:51.590 --> 01:56.390
<b>Mysterious Disappearances</b>

03:17.670 --> 03:23.140
<b>A Book, an Affair, and a Mermaid</b>

03:17.670 --> 03:23.140
<b>A Book, an Affair, and a Mermaid</b>

03:19.470 --> 03:22.130
I got three cans of coffee out of him.

03:34.610 --> 03:36.150
<i>When I was a child,</i>

03:36.860 --> 03:39.400
<i>I had severe trouble talking to others.</i>

03:39.840 --> 03:42.470
<i>It wasn't that I had nothing I wanted to say—</i>

03:42.470 --> 03:43.970
<i>rather, the opposite.</i>

03:44.420 --> 03:46.910
<i>Extreme heat melted the words into goo.</i>

03:46.910 --> 03:49.970
<i>They'd blend together in my head 
so that I couldn't get them out,</i>

03:49.970 --> 03:51.630
<i>and I'd have no idea what to do.</i>

03:51.630 --> 03:52.870
Tell me why!

03:52.140 --> 03:56.380
<i>That particular day, something trivial had 
turned into a fight with my friends again.</i>

03:52.870 --> 03:54.280
Come on!

03:56.930 --> 03:59.250
<i>I wasn't able to defend myself or apologize.</i>

03:59.250 --> 04:01.390
<i>All I could do was stay sullenly silent.</i>

04:01.390 --> 04:03.060
Why aren't you saying anything?

04:03.060 --> 04:05.270
Come on! Say something!

04:03.430 --> 04:06.140
<i>That made my friends angrier,</i>

04:06.140 --> 04:08.150
<i>and things only worsened from there.</i>

04:11.670 --> 04:13.270
Wait, Sumireko-chan!

04:13.270 --> 04:14.530
Stop right there!

04:14.530 --> 04:15.870
Wait!

04:35.420 --> 04:36.500
Sumireko-chan!

04:36.500 --> 04:38.050
Where did you go?

04:54.110 --> 04:57.820
<b>Gyokushin-dou Bookstore</b>

05:20.410 --> 05:21.990
Close the door.

05:24.650 --> 05:28.220
Sunlight is a book's worst enemy, young lady.

05:28.220 --> 05:30.230
I-I'm so sorry!

05:33.620 --> 05:35.110
Browse at your leisure.

05:59.980 --> 06:01.540
What are you looking at?

06:02.310 --> 06:06.010
Reading a good book can 
make one cry, can't it?

06:06.010 --> 06:07.800
Even a grown-up?

06:09.050 --> 06:12.520
Now, look here. Adults cry 
when they need to cry, too.

06:12.520 --> 06:14.270
Like when reading a book?

06:14.270 --> 06:15.530
That's right.

06:15.530 --> 06:18.530
If you're interested, 
why don't you give it a read?

06:22.530 --> 06:23.800
You don't mind?

06:24.150 --> 06:27.030
As it happens, I've just finished it.

06:28.660 --> 06:32.780
There's a space in the back for people to 
read before they buy. Use it as you wish.

06:32.780 --> 06:35.420
There won't likely be any 
other customers, anyway.

06:55.190 --> 06:57.810
Books are so boring, though.

07:12.840 --> 07:17.330
<i>"Right now, I have three 
envelopes in front of me.</i>

07:17.730 --> 07:23.840
<i>However, I am only allowed 
to open and read one of them.</i>

07:24.260 --> 07:28.310
<i>Which envelope holds the 
message that person wrote?</i>

07:28.310 --> 07:31.330
<i>All of the envelopes are exactly the same,</i>

07:31.330 --> 07:35.010
<i>but on the outside of one, 
I can see an ever-so-faint stain.</i>

07:35.690 --> 07:40.970
<i>I pick that one up and open it without 
giving myself the chance to waver.</i>

07:41.590 --> 07:45.190
<i>I pull out the sheet of paper inside, 
and the instant I lay eyes on its message,</i>

07:45.190 --> 07:48.900
<i>the world around me changes completely."</i>

07:50.620 --> 07:54.710
It's sunset. You should go home. 
Your family will be worried.

07:54.710 --> 07:55.810
But...

07:55.810 --> 07:58.180
You can continue tomorrow.

07:58.180 --> 07:59.510
But...

08:02.380 --> 08:06.380
All right, then, I'll lend you 
this book. However...

08:06.380 --> 08:09.090
You must be certain to come back 
and return it tomorrow.

08:09.500 --> 08:10.390
I will!

08:20.770 --> 08:23.270
<b>Gyokushin-dou Bookstore</b>

08:23.270 --> 08:24.380
Here.

08:24.770 --> 08:28.200
You brought it back. Thank you.

08:30.810 --> 08:34.590
Why don't you pick a book 
from over there to read next?

08:41.040 --> 08:44.040
<i>"The coffee had long since gone cold.</i>

08:44.360 --> 08:48.800
<i>'Say, do you love your wife?' asked Julia.</i>

08:48.800 --> 08:54.050
<i>Behind me, the door opened and another 
customer came in, but I paid it no heed.</i>

08:54.050 --> 08:56.800
<i>Helplessly, I answered without thinking.</i>

08:56.800 --> 08:58.800
<i>'Of course I don't love her.'</i>

08:58.800 --> 09:02.650
<i>In that moment, Julia looked 
behind me and smiled, just a little.</i>

09:02.650 --> 09:06.220
<i>When I turned around, 
I realized that it was all over.</i>

09:06.220 --> 09:08.070
<i>The person standing there was my wife.</i>

09:08.560 --> 09:12.320
<i>And from behind the frozen mask 
of her expression, she said,</i>

09:12.710 --> 09:14.780
<i>'I hope you'll be very happy.'"</i>

09:15.830 --> 09:16.960
Say...

09:16.960 --> 09:17.830
Hm?

09:17.830 --> 09:19.830
What's an "affair"?

09:19.830 --> 09:23.860
An affair? Well, let's see...

09:24.300 --> 09:30.340
It's something that everyone does, but it's 
the absolute worst thing to get caught doing.

09:30.650 --> 09:31.900
Really?

09:31.900 --> 09:36.350
So it's like underlining all the 
dirty words in the dictionary?

09:36.350 --> 09:40.760
There's nothing wrong with doing that, 
but is it something you do?

09:40.760 --> 09:43.180
My heart is filled with worry.

09:43.180 --> 09:45.110
The boys in class do it.

09:45.110 --> 09:47.690
My, they're incorrigible.

09:48.600 --> 09:51.610
<i>"The man spoke with a sneer.</i>

09:51.930 --> 09:56.150
<i>'Magic? You still believe 
in magic at your age?'</i>

09:56.150 --> 09:58.770
<i>Sally bit her lip in frustration.</i>

09:58.770 --> 10:02.780
<i>It was as though he'd read 
her mind once again.</i>

10:03.200 --> 10:06.380
<i>An urge to kill him welled up inside her.</i>

10:06.380 --> 10:11.380
<i>'It isn't ghosts who curse people,' 
said the medium.</i>

10:11.380 --> 10:16.360
<i>'Before she took her own life, 
she said something to you, didn't she?'</i>

10:16.360 --> 10:18.920
<i>'She told you the name of a flower.'</i>

10:18.920 --> 10:21.140
<i>The medium's narrowed eyes opened wide.</i>

10:21.140 --> 10:24.650
<i>'That is what a curse is.'"</i>

10:34.920 --> 10:36.780
What kind of book is this?

10:36.780 --> 10:38.520
You have a good eye.

10:38.520 --> 10:42.230
That's the type of mystery that 
would knock a sumo wrestler dead.

10:42.910 --> 10:44.290
That sounds like fun!

10:47.570 --> 10:50.090
You seem like you'll be all right now.

10:55.800 --> 10:58.950
I think I'll ask after all, just the once.

10:59.680 --> 11:02.430
Did something happen to you?

11:02.430 --> 11:03.390
Huh?

11:03.930 --> 11:07.850
Well, the first time you came 
in here, you were crying.

11:08.430 --> 11:10.830
You don't have to tell me 
if you don't want to.

11:10.830 --> 11:13.030
It's not like me to ask, anyway.

11:18.110 --> 11:19.950
I-I-I...

11:20.770 --> 11:22.680
<i>My difficulty in speaking to others,</i>

11:23.200 --> 11:25.960
<i>and how it caused fights 
between me and my friends...</i>

11:26.780 --> 11:31.940
<i>I remember that explaining just those 
two things took an agonizingly long time.</i>

11:34.190 --> 11:39.670
<i>But she didn't try to hurry me. 
She waited patiently for me to finish.</i>

11:45.100 --> 11:47.710
Do you want to get better 
at speaking to people?

11:47.710 --> 11:48.980
Yes.

11:49.310 --> 11:50.870
Did you know?

11:50.870 --> 11:54.970
Everyone has a dictionary inside their head.

11:54.970 --> 11:57.500
It's called your inner dictionary.

11:57.500 --> 12:01.120
We unconsciously search through it 
for words when we speak.

12:01.810 --> 12:06.200
It seems you're not very good 
at pulling out that dictionary.

12:11.600 --> 12:13.430
Try writing a passage.

12:18.600 --> 12:20.980
You don't have to physically write it.

12:20.980 --> 12:24.400
First, ready some manuscript 
paper in your head.

12:25.140 --> 12:29.390
Write down all the words you 
know and give them form.

12:30.340 --> 12:33.280
You're going to take the dictionary 
in your head apart.

12:33.280 --> 12:35.400
You already have all the words you need.

12:36.160 --> 12:39.210
Next, rearrange the words 
on the manuscript paper,

12:39.210 --> 12:40.410
and turn them into writing.

12:40.880 --> 12:44.160
Then try reading the 
manuscript you've created.

12:46.090 --> 12:47.810
Do you think I can do it?

12:47.810 --> 12:48.960
I know you can.

12:50.250 --> 12:52.420
Practice makes perfect, though.

12:53.340 --> 12:58.350
I'm certain that you will write 
a vast number of passages.

12:58.350 --> 13:00.930
Your everyday life is in itself 
a full-length novel.

13:03.440 --> 13:08.620
One day, when you write a novel, 
I hope you'll let me read it.

13:09.100 --> 13:12.800
I've already read nearly 
all the books, after all.

13:09.940 --> 13:14.440
<b>Gyokushin-dou Bookstore</b>

13:12.800 --> 13:13.820
I will!

13:15.440 --> 13:17.940
<b>This kanji...
...is here</b>

13:17.940 --> 13:19.630
Hey, watch this!

13:20.840 --> 13:23.200
<i>Her advice worked immediately.</i>

13:23.620 --> 13:26.200
<i>I was soon able to speak with my friends.</i>

13:30.310 --> 13:34.730
<i>I headed to the bookstore to thank her
for helping me make up with them,</i>

13:38.210 --> 13:42.310
<i>but I was never able to find 
the path there again.</i>

13:46.270 --> 13:49.720
<i>Now, that path... 
was right in front of my eyes.</i>

14:00.490 --> 14:01.610
Crap...

14:05.220 --> 14:09.140
"I regret that with each 
new day, you grow older."

14:18.190 --> 14:19.750
Piece of cake.

14:35.100 --> 14:38.730
<b>Gyokushin-dou Bookstore</b>

14:45.260 --> 14:50.290
And so, the truth of the store where 
I read those books remains yet unknown.

14:50.290 --> 14:52.010
That's how the story ends.

14:52.790 --> 14:57.520
I see. Well, that solves one 
of the mysteries about you.

14:57.520 --> 15:00.550
About me? Details needed.

15:00.550 --> 15:05.550
I've always felt that the way you speak 
is unusual. There's a certain stiffness.

15:05.550 --> 15:10.060
Oh. It naturally turned out that 
way due to how I write as I speak.

15:10.500 --> 15:14.180
I've taken a liking to this 
way of speaking, though.

15:14.180 --> 15:16.560
It sort of sounds like her, in a way.

15:16.560 --> 15:17.820
Really?

15:17.820 --> 15:20.250
That's kind of an understated reaction.

15:20.250 --> 15:24.090
More importantly, do you remember 
the name of that bookstore?

15:24.090 --> 15:25.570
The name?

15:26.190 --> 15:29.510
For some reason, only vaguely.

15:29.510 --> 15:33.010
I'm pretty certain it was 
something like Gyokushin-dou.

15:33.010 --> 15:34.830
Gyokushin-dou?

15:35.470 --> 15:36.800
What is it?

15:36.800 --> 15:39.430
A mysterious woman who loves books...

15:39.880 --> 15:43.090
Did she invite you in, thinking you 
two were birds of a feather?

15:43.460 --> 15:44.270
Hey.

15:44.270 --> 15:45.340
Ogawa-san!

15:45.340 --> 15:46.250
Yes, sir?

15:46.250 --> 15:50.460
We don't have many customers right now, 
so could you help me bring in the delivery?

15:50.460 --> 15:51.920
Yes, of course.

15:51.920 --> 15:53.630
Sorry to ask, but thanks.

15:57.010 --> 15:59.030
So, about our conversation earlier...

15:59.030 --> 16:01.860
Yes, regarding the mysterious woman.

16:02.370 --> 16:05.860
This story dates back to the time 
of the fifth shogun, Tsunayoshi.

16:06.680 --> 16:09.480
There once was a beautiful 
woman of noble birth.

16:09.480 --> 16:13.160
It's said that she was such an avid reader, 
she twice reread the more than five thousand

16:13.160 --> 16:15.080
scrolls of Buddhist scripture.

16:15.390 --> 16:17.560
After the lady's death, 
in accordance with her will,

16:17.560 --> 16:19.440
her books were donated to the temple.

16:19.440 --> 16:24.220
A storehouse for them was built next to her 
grave so that anyone could borrow them.

16:24.840 --> 16:27.880
It's said that if you put your ear 
to the lady's grave in Yotsuya,

16:27.880 --> 16:30.510
you could hear a voice reading books.

16:31.160 --> 16:34.760
According to one theory, her posthumous 
Buddhist name was Gyokushin-in.

16:33.680 --> 16:34.760
<b>m 0 0 l 100 0 100 100 0 100</b>

16:33.800 --> 16:34.760
<b>Gyokushin-in</b>

16:34.760 --> 16:36.020
What?

16:36.720 --> 16:39.330
Because she loved reading so much, 
she was given the nickname...

16:39.310 --> 16:42.430
<b>\h\h\hHonhime\h\h\h
(The Lady of Books)</b>

16:39.780 --> 16:41.020
Honhime.

16:41.020 --> 16:42.540
"Honhime"?

16:42.540 --> 16:46.280
There was one rule for borrowing 
books from the lady's library.

16:46.750 --> 16:50.140
"When returning a book, 
you must add one more."

16:50.140 --> 16:52.730
Those who didn't follow it 
met with ill fortune.

16:53.110 --> 16:55.910
She must have really 
wanted to read new books.

16:55.910 --> 16:59.760
Huh? But I never added any myself.

17:00.670 --> 17:05.920
One day, when you write a novel, 
I hope you'll let me read it.

17:05.920 --> 17:09.920
I see. There are some very 
patient readers out there.

17:09.920 --> 17:10.870
Huh?

17:14.280 --> 17:18.440
The next time I see her, I'll have 
to present her with a new book...

17:18.830 --> 17:22.560
signed by the author Ogawa Sumireko.

17:27.440 --> 17:28.620
<i>Can you hear me?</i>

17:28.620 --> 17:34.450
<i>To all you small fry out there, this is 
the mermaid VTuber, Himeuo Yorumun!</i>

17:35.690 --> 17:36.660
For you.

17:36.660 --> 17:38.450
Oh, how very considerate.

17:44.260 --> 17:46.910
Any leads on new cursed objects?

17:46.910 --> 17:48.850
It's difficult.

17:48.850 --> 17:52.970
There isn't necessarily a cursed object 
associated with every Curiosity.

17:52.970 --> 17:55.060
Is that how it works?

17:55.060 --> 17:56.720
That's how it works.

17:56.720 --> 18:01.910
Then wouldn't it be faster to seek out 
a physical Curiosity from the start?

18:01.910 --> 18:03.230
Like a tsukumogami or something.

18:03.230 --> 18:08.130
A tsukumogami, huh? 
Finding one of those at all is difficult.

18:08.130 --> 18:09.410
Is it?

18:09.410 --> 18:13.490
I can't exactly go snooping through people's 
personal items without permission, can I?

18:13.490 --> 18:16.740
Sumireko-san, will you show me 
all of your personal items?

18:16.740 --> 18:18.910
Get out of here and never come back.

18:18.910 --> 18:19.990
Right?

18:19.990 --> 18:24.380
And before that, I have to wonder 
if tsukumogami even exist in this era.

18:24.380 --> 18:26.880
What? This coming from you of all people?

18:26.880 --> 18:30.510
These days, everything is 
made to be thrown away.

18:30.800 --> 18:35.010
If anything, digital data is treated 
with more care than physical items.

18:35.010 --> 18:40.480
I see. It <i>is</i> hard to imagine a more difficult
environment for the creation of tsukumogami.

18:43.870 --> 18:45.020
Say, Occhan!

18:45.020 --> 18:47.150
Have you been studying for finals?

18:47.150 --> 18:48.670
Not at all.

18:48.670 --> 18:52.030
You're kidding! 
But you always get good grades!

18:52.030 --> 18:55.530
I really haven't studied, though. 
What about you, Nodoka?

18:55.530 --> 19:00.300
I've got a home tutor assigned 
to me. I really hate it.

19:02.460 --> 19:05.810
But you know what? There's something 
I do in secret that's a lot of fun.

19:07.230 --> 19:08.040
What is it?

19:08.870 --> 19:11.050
Collecting cute underwear.

19:11.050 --> 19:12.300
Really?

19:12.300 --> 19:14.790
Yup. See?

19:15.560 --> 19:18.550
You shouldn't do that, Nodoka. It's unseemly.

19:18.550 --> 19:21.660
You'll end up like that 
slovenly apartment wife.

19:21.660 --> 19:24.060
Ahpartmentwaif? Who's that?

19:24.360 --> 19:26.620
It's Wakazukuri-senpai's real name.

19:26.620 --> 19:28.470
It is?

19:28.470 --> 19:33.380
You know, I always thought 
her name sounded a bit odd!

19:33.380 --> 19:36.320
So it's a stage name, right?

19:36.630 --> 19:40.640
Well, how about that! So her real 
name is Ahpartmentwaif-senpai!

19:40.970 --> 19:42.080
Y-Yes.

19:42.080 --> 19:47.040
Hey, how about we get everyone together 
to study at my place before finals?

19:47.040 --> 19:48.220
You don't mind me coming over?

19:48.220 --> 19:52.250
Of course not! I'm all alone on 
Saturdays, so I get super bored.

19:52.250 --> 19:53.590
Sure. Sounds like fun.

19:53.970 --> 19:56.590
Bye, Occhan!

19:56.590 --> 19:58.090
Bye-bye!

20:10.860 --> 20:13.560
<b>Takamanohara Hospital</b>

20:21.980 --> 20:23.120
Hello.

20:23.860 --> 20:27.000
I've been waiting for you, Adashino Oto.

20:28.150 --> 20:31.100
Welcome home, masters.

20:31.100 --> 20:32.750
My name is Otohime.

20:32.750 --> 20:35.230
Where are you from?

20:35.230 --> 20:39.850
I have wandered into this land 
from a certain small country.

20:39.850 --> 20:42.860
But I believe that one day, 
I will be able to return home.

20:42.860 --> 20:44.760
So cute!

20:45.990 --> 20:49.520
Today, I drew all my favorite animals.

20:49.520 --> 20:51.290
So cute!

20:51.290 --> 20:53.770
You're much too kind, masters.

21:05.900 --> 21:08.020
This is no fun!

21:09.960 --> 21:12.710
The school and Dad are way too mean!

21:12.710 --> 21:16.590
<b>m 0 0 l 100 0 100 100 0 100</b>

21:12.710 --> 21:16.590
<b>\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\h\hTo Students and Parents

Creating social media accounts or accounts
on video sites for the purpose of hosting
livestreams or uploading videos is prohibited.

Livestreams and/or videos can only be watched
with a parent or guardian's permission,
subject to a daily viewing limit at their discretion.</b>

21:12.710 --> 21:16.590
I just want everyone 
to see me sing and dance!

21:22.160 --> 21:23.470
Oh, no!

21:29.590 --> 21:30.730
<i>Can you hear me?</i>

21:30.730 --> 21:37.210
<i>To all you small fry out there, this is 
